1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,233
A charity poster shows a country in one word: poverty.

2
00:00:04,233 --> 00:00:06,095
Hunger, disaster, need.

3
00:00:06,095 --> 00:00:11,160
Ben Okri's poem argues that this picture is not just incomplete.

4
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:12,215
It is part of the problem.

5
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:19,961
There's more to a people than their poverty, he writes: there is their work, their wisdom, their creativity.

6
00:00:19,961 --> 00:00:23,355
Change how people are seen, and you change what is possible.

7
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:34,773
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 11: The Awakening Age, by Ben Okri.

8
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:38,262
Six parts.

9
00:00:38,262 --> 00:00:40,906
Ben Okri and the poem's occasion.

10
00:00:40,906 --> 00:00:42,051
The summons.

11
00:00:42,051 --> 00:00:42,872
The rope.

12
00:00:42,872 --> 00:00:44,734
Perception and harvest.

13
00:00:44,734 --> 00:00:45,932
The themes.

14
00:00:45,932 --> 00:00:47,432
And the form and devices.

15
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:58,852
Ben Okri was born in Nigeria in nineteen fifty nine; your textbook's nineteen forty nine is a misprint.

16
00:00:58,852 --> 00:01:04,217
He spent his early childhood in London, then returned to Nigeria during its civil war.

17
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:09,269
His novel The Famished Road won the Booker Prize when he was only thirty two.

18
00:01:09,269 --> 00:01:19,555
Critics call him a magical realist; he replies that in his tradition there are simply more dimensions to reality: myths, ancestors, spirits.

19
00:01:19,555 --> 00:01:21,285
He was knighted in twenty twenty three.

20
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,184
Here is context almost no guide gives you.

21
00:01:25,184 --> 00:01:40,288
The poem was commissioned in nineteen ninety nine by Oxfam for a millennium project called On The Line, which linked schools and communities along the Greenwich Meridian, the line of zero longitude, from Britain down through France, Spain, and West Africa.

22
00:01:40,288 --> 00:01:44,780
That explains the first line: O ye who travel the meridian line.

23
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:53,179
So it is not a war poem, and not really about labour migration: its subject is rich and poor countries at the end of the century.

24
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:57,039
Give that fact in one sentence, then answer the question asked.

25
00:02:01,833 --> 00:02:03,957
Couplet one opens like a blessing.

26
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:13,319
O ye who travel the meridian line: O ye is the old language of the Bible and public prayer, lifting the poem into ceremony.

27
00:02:13,319 --> 00:02:15,871
Then may: the grammar of wishing.

28
00:02:15,871 --> 00:02:21,496
Six of the seven couplets are wishes or statements; not one is an order.

29
00:02:21,496 --> 00:02:27,017
The vision of a new world is to shine within you, so change begins inside.

30
00:02:27,017 --> 00:02:30,794
And shine starts a chain of light running through the whole poem.

31
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,030
Couplet two.

32
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:38,890
Eyes that have lived with poverty's rage: not sadness, rage, violent anger.

33
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:40,556
Okri will not soften it.

34
00:02:40,556 --> 00:02:45,088
And they are to see through to the glory of the awakening age.

35
00:02:45,088 --> 00:02:50,309
See through: poverty is like a fog the vision must pierce.

36
00:02:50,309 --> 00:02:55,830
And awakening, not new: the light was always there, and the eyes were shut.

37
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:07,268
Couplet three gives the reason, beginning with for, the only logical connective in the poem.

38
00:03:07,268 --> 00:03:14,925
We are richly linked in hope: richly, just after poverty, relocates wealth into connection.

39
00:03:14,925 --> 00:03:17,776
Woven in history, like a mountain rope.

40
00:03:17,776 --> 00:03:21,083
Think about a climbing rope, as a Nepali reader can.

41
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:24,104
It is many strands twisted into one.

42
00:03:24,104 --> 00:03:27,190
It is useless unless tied at both ends.

43
00:03:27,190 --> 00:03:29,482
The climbers share one fate.

44
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:32,162
And it exists for one purpose: going up.

45
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:35,517
Couplet four uses the rope.

46
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:39,931
Together, the first word, is the poem's whole condition.

47
00:03:39,931 --> 00:03:47,314
Ascend is a formal, aspiring verb: you ascend a mountain, a throne, a stair to heaven.

48
00:03:47,314 --> 00:03:52,288
And the guide is our heart's clearest light, not policy or economics.

49
00:03:52,288 --> 00:03:56,168
Notice our heart: one heart, many owners.

50
00:03:56,168 --> 00:03:57,987
The many have become one climber.

51
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:09,995
Couplet five is the intellectual centre, the lines to quote if you must defend this as a serious poem.

52
00:04:09,995 --> 00:04:16,271
What must change first is not aid, not the economy, but perception: how people are seen.

53
00:04:16,271 --> 00:04:28,016
The passive, perceptions are changed, leaves open who changes them: the poor, who see themselves through others' eyes, and the rich, who see only a problem to pity.

54
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:35,210
Then a flowering of truth instead of pain: much of the pain, it suggests, was the pain of being misdescribed.

55
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:45,979
Couplet six is the one to memorise: there's more to a people than their poverty; there is their work, their wisdom, their creativity.

56
00:04:45,979 --> 00:04:53,414
Work comes first: this is not sentimental noble poverty, but an economy the poster never photographs.

57
00:04:53,414 --> 00:05:03,018
And the rhyme, poverty with creativity, the poem's only long rhyme, chains together exactly the two words the couplet is arguing about.

58
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:09,560
The last couplet turns the poem's technique into its meaning: may our lives rhyme.

59
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:21,539
A rhyme is two different words that answer each other, not identical, but in harmony: that is Okri's model of how people should relate, and he has spent thirteen lines demonstrating it in couplets.

60
00:05:21,539 --> 00:05:25,472
Along the line returns to the meridian, closing the circle.

61
00:05:25,472 --> 00:05:35,044
And a loving harvest of space and time: something sown long ago, patient, and enough for everyone, the answer to poverty.

62
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:40,772
Themes.

63
00:05:40,772 --> 00:05:48,611
Perception, not charity: describing people only by what they lack is itself part of what keeps them poor.

64
00:05:48,611 --> 00:05:53,598
Solidarity: richly linked in hope, one rope, one fate.

65
00:05:53,598 --> 00:05:56,006
Hope and awakening, beginning within.

66
00:05:56,006 --> 00:06:01,944
And the dignity of the poor: their work, wisdom and creativity, not our pity.

67
00:06:01,944 --> 00:06:06,822
You may apply the poem to Nepali workers abroad, but say honestly that you are applying it.

68
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:12,500
The form.

69
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:20,026
Fourteen lines, which is why your exercises call it a sonnet, but there is no regular metre and no sonnet rhyme scheme.

70
00:06:20,026 --> 00:06:26,836
Instead, seven rhyming couplets: a a, b b, c c, and so on.

71
00:06:26,836 --> 00:06:35,168
The couplets are the argument: pairs of lines that answer one another, until the last couplet names the idea: may our lives rhyme.

72
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:37,899
Devices to quote.

73
00:06:37,899 --> 00:06:40,594
Apostrophe: O ye who travel.

74
00:06:40,594 --> 00:06:42,573
The simile of the mountain rope.

75
00:06:42,573 --> 00:06:45,725
Metaphor: a flowering of truth.

76
00:06:45,725 --> 00:06:47,613
A chain of light imagery.

77
00:06:47,613 --> 00:06:50,738
Antithesis: truth instead of pain.

78
00:06:50,738 --> 00:06:52,847
And the closing harvest metaphor.

79
00:06:55,267 --> 00:06:56,037
Pause and think.

80
00:07:03,333 --> 00:07:09,844
aa bb cc dd ee ff gg: seven couplets, fourteen lines.

81
00:07:11,833 --> 00:07:12,268
One more.

82
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:23,659
"When perceptions are changed", truth flowers instead of pain.

83
00:07:25,833 --> 00:07:26,889
Quick recap.

84
00:07:26,889 --> 00:07:30,600
Okri, and a poem written for a line round the earth.

85
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,462
A blessing in wishes.

86
00:07:32,462 --> 00:07:34,728
Poverty's rage seen through.

87
00:07:34,728 --> 00:07:36,434
A mountain rope of hope.

88
00:07:36,434 --> 00:07:38,478
Perception before charity.

89
00:07:38,478 --> 00:07:41,519
And seven couplets that ask our lives to rhyme.

90
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,525
That was The Awakening Age.

91
00:07:47,525 --> 00:07:50,441
Next, the last poem: Soft Storm.

92
00:07:50,441 --> 00:07:51,012
See you there.

